; Module boundary contracts — SRS §6.5.
;
;   "Discipline that is only documented is not discipline."
;
; These contracts encode the dependency table of SRS §6.4 and the layer
; structure of SRS §8.2. A forbidden import fails CI (SRS §6.5 rule 2).
;
; PROVENANCE. This table was first transcribed from a `pdftotext` extraction of
; the SRS. `-layout` column-scrambles multi-column tables, so on 2026-08-18 it
; was re-derived from the authoritative .docx by reading the OOXML table grid
; directly (scripts/extract_srs.py). All 42 cells were compared: no
; transcription error, and the only difference is the deliberate S3 divergence
; noted below. tests/test_srs_module_catalogue.py now re-derives §6.4 from
; docs/srs/srs-docx.txt on every run and fails if any contract here drifts from
; it — so this comment can no longer go stale without CI saying so.
;
; The dependency table, verbatim from SRS §6.4 (v1.1, pp. 21-22):
;
;   identity       -> (none)
;   location       -> (none; external port only)
;   notify         -> (none; external ports only)
;   catalogue      -> location
;   provider       -> identity
;   inventory      -> catalogue
;   transport      -> location, provider
;   trip           -> catalogue, transport
;   booking        -> inventory, trip, provider
;   payment        -> booking            [see PAYMENT NOTE below]
;   messaging      -> booking
;   review         -> booking
;   finance        -> payment, booking
;   administration -> all (read via interfaces)
;
; Dependencies are read STRICTLY and NON-TRANSITIVELY. `inventory` depends on
; `catalogue`, and `catalogue` depends on `location`, but `inventory` may not
; import `location` directly — it goes through `catalogue`'s service interface.
; That is what makes the seams extractable later (SRS §6.2, §44.2).
;
; Every module may import `apps.common` and `ports`, which are leaves.
;
; Every `deps-*` contract below sets `allow_indirect_imports = True`, which
; makes it check DIRECT imports only. That is what "read STRICTLY and
; NON-TRANSITIVELY" above means, and without it the contracts contradict the
; graph they encode: `booking -> trip` is legal and `trip -> catalogue` is
; legal, so `booking` reaches `catalogue` through a chain of two permitted
; edges — which is the transitive closure of a legal DAG, not a violation.
;
; Left unset, the first module to actually use one of its declared
; dependencies fails the contract for its dependency's dependencies. That did
; not happen until Phase 5 because nothing above L2 had imported anything yet;
; `booking.services` calling `inventory.services` and `trip.services` is the
; first time an L4 module used the triple §6.4 gave it.
;
; The privacy contracts have carried the same flag since ADR 0014, for the
; same reason: what is being forbidden is an import somebody wrote, not a
; module somebody can reach.
;
; PAYMENT NOTE. SRS §6.4 gives payment's dependency as "booking (via events)".
; That is not implementable as written: §9.4.7 asserts `trip.status` and reads
; `trip.total_amount` synchronously, and §20.8's confirmation routine commits
; holds and confirms bookings inside the webhook transaction. The contract
; below therefore permits booking, trip and inventory. Revisit at Phase 8 —
; issue S3 in docs/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md.

[importlinter]
root_packages =
    apps
    config
    ports
include_external_packages = True

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. Module dependency contracts — one per module, encoding SRS §6.4.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

